1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,056
Somewhere out there, right
now, it is still 1990.

2
00:00:06,456 --> 00:00:08,673
Light doesn't arrive all at once.

3
00:00:08,673 --> 00:00:18,309
It travels. And every moment that has ever happened on Earth is still
travelling outward, at three hundred thousand kilometres a second.

4
00:00:19,008 --> 00:00:23,467
So let's chase it. And at every star
we pass, we'll ask one question:

5
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:26,164
what do they see of us, right now?

6
00:00:27,364 --> 00:00:31,595
The Moon sees Earth as
it was 1.3 seconds ago.

7
00:00:32,395 --> 00:00:35,739
The Sun, 8 minutes and 20 seconds ago.

8
00:00:36,739 --> 00:00:40,582
Six billion kilometres out,
Earth is a pale blue dot.

9
00:00:40,582 --> 00:00:43,910
Then it's lost in the glare of the Sun.

10
00:00:45,610 --> 00:00:52,870
Proxima Centauri, our nearest star, 4.2
light-years away, sees the summer of 2022.

11
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:57,797
The James Webb telescope
has just opened its eyes.

12
00:00:58,897 --> 00:01:03,780
Sirius, the brightest star
in our night sky: early 2018.

13
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:13,371
Vega, 25 light-years: late 2001. The space
station has had people aboard for about a year.

14
00:01:14,571 --> 00:01:16,506
Arcturus, 37 light-years.

15
00:01:16,506 --> 00:01:28,693
And here is something strange. Arcturus is seeing Earth from early 1990,
around the very month Voyager turned back and photographed that pale blue dot.

16
00:01:30,593 --> 00:01:38,815
Caph, in Cassiopeia, 55 light-years: 1972.
The last year anyone walked on the Moon.

17
00:01:40,315 --> 00:01:44,166
Around 100 light-years, we
cross the edge of something:

18
00:01:44,166 --> 00:01:56,498
our first radio broadcasts, from the 1920s. Beyond this line, no one could have heard
us. And even inside it, our signals are almost certainly far too faint to pick out.

19
00:01:57,998 --> 00:02:01,311
Dubhe, in the Big Dipper, 123 light-years.

20
00:02:01,311 --> 00:02:09,147
Give or take a couple of years, the first
aeroplane is lifting off a beach in North Carolina.

21
00:02:10,447 --> 00:02:14,521
Polaris, the North Star,
about 446 light-years:

22
00:02:14,521 --> 00:02:24,641
around 1580. Ships are crossing oceans, steering by this
very star. No one has ever looked through a telescope.

23
00:02:25,941 --> 00:02:33,825
Betelgeuse, roughly 550 light-years, though
that distance is uncertain by a hundred or so.

24
00:02:33,825 --> 00:02:40,983
It sees the 1400s, or the 1500s.
Cathedrals, and the first printing presses.

25
00:02:42,283 --> 00:02:45,725
The Orion Nebula, about 1,300 light-years:

26
00:02:45,725 --> 00:02:50,569
around the year 700. Maya
cities are at their height.

27
00:02:51,669 --> 00:02:53,299
Deneb is a mystery.

28
00:02:53,299 --> 00:03:03,751
It might be 1,400 light-years away, or 2,600. Either way,
it sees the classical world. Rome rising, or Rome falling.

29
00:03:05,051 --> 00:03:08,530
VY Canis Majoris, about 3,900 light-years.

30
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:18,720
The Great Pyramid is already centuries old. And on an island
in the Arctic, the last woolly mammoths are still alive.

31
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:24,650
Of course, no one out there
could really watch any of this.

32
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:39,289
To make out details just a couple of kilometres across from Proxima Centauri, you would need a telescope
as wide as the Earth. From farther away, wider still. The light is there. Seeing it is another matter.

33
00:03:40,989 --> 00:03:42,442
27,000 light-years:

34
00:03:42,442 --> 00:03:53,346
the heart of our galaxy. Earth is in an ice age. Glaciers bury much
of the northern continents, and mammoths roam the plains below them.

35
00:03:54,746 --> 00:03:59,178
The Large Magellanic
Cloud, 160,000 light-years.

36
00:03:59,178 --> 00:04:05,034
Our species is young, and lives
almost entirely in Africa.

37
00:04:06,234 --> 00:04:12,384
And Andromeda, 2.5 million light-years
away, sees an Earth with no humans at all.

38
00:04:12,384 --> 00:04:17,895
Only our distant ancestors, shaping
some of the first stone tools.

39
00:04:19,795 --> 00:04:24,491
66 million light-years out,
the asteroid is still falling.

40
00:04:26,991 --> 00:04:30,784
Everything we've ever done
is still travelling outward.

41
00:04:31,684 --> 00:04:33,846
Somewhere, it's still happening.

